To empirically showcase the efficacy of \ours{}, we conduct a thorough evaluation by comparing its results against both a centralized setting and non-collaborating clients. Additionally, we scrutinize naive averaging-based and proximity-based aggregation methods discussed earlier, providing specific insights into the limitations of the former when confronted with vertical data distributions. Finally, we conclude our assessment with scalability experiments and a comparative analysis of \ours{} against selected baselines in the literature on both real-world and synthetic graphs. 

