\subsubsection{Training Object of QueryGraphormer}
Finally, we feed the representations of $g_h$ and $g_r$ in the last layer, denoted as $\boldsymbol{g_h}, \boldsymbol{g_r} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_1}$, into the pretrained neural  link predictor to obtain predicted answers. 
Formally, given a query graph $g$, the score of each tail entity $t_i$ is calculated as follows:
\begin{gather}
    s(t_i \, | \, g)= \phi(\boldsymbol{g_h}', \boldsymbol{g_r}', \boldsymbol{t_i}) \\
    \boldsymbol{g_h}' = MLP_{rev}(\boldsymbol{g_h}), \, \boldsymbol{g_r}' = MLP_{rev}(\boldsymbol{g_r})
\end{gather}
where $MLP_{rev}$ projects the representations from $d_1$ dimension back to $d_0$ dimension, $\boldsymbol{t_i} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_0}$ is the pretrained embedding of entity $t_i$, $\phi$ is the score function of the pretrained KGE.

It is noteworthy that $\boldsymbol{t_i}$ is from the pretrained entity embeddings, and they are not needed to updated in the training of QueryGraphormer, which effectively reduces the number of parameters needed to be updated.

Our training object is to maximize the log probabilities of correct answer $t$, the loss function (without label smoothing for simplicity) is defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
    L = -(s(t \, | \, g) - log \sum_{i=1}^K \exp \left[s(t_i \, | \, g)\right])
\end{equation}
where $t$ and $t_i$ are the answer entity and negative entity (random sampling), respectively.

