\subsection{Evaluation on benchmark datasets}
In this study, our primary focus is on  generating accurate expression skeletons, which are crucial part for deriving  physical relationships. It is important to recognize that while various combinations of operator terms can yield a high coefficient of determination, this does not inherently assure the precision of the final predictions or the correctness of the ultimate expression \cite{li2022transformer}. We evaluate the skeleton recovery rate using the challenging test set proposed by \citet{li2022transformer}, which consists of the same skeletons as in the training set but with different numerical coefficients, named SSDNC dataset~\cite{li2022transformer}. SSDNC dataset is currently the most comprehensive dataset, containing 963 unseen equations. Further details about the SSDNC dataset can be found in Appendix \ref{a.dataset}. Our method is compared against three state-of-the-art (SOTA)  transformer-based supervised learning methods, SymPointMLP~\cite{li2022transformer}, SymbolicGPT~\cite{valipour2021symbolicgpt}, and NeSymReS~\cite{biggio2021neural}. Our method achieves a notable recovery rate of \textbf{42.7$\%$}  without the beam search and an impressive \textbf{82.3$\%$}  with the implementation of the beam search strategy. As detailed in Table \ref{main_result}, our proposed method surpasses the SOTA methods by up to $32.0\%$. Furthermore, it outperforms these methods in terms of average $R^2$, achieving a remarkable score of \textbf{0.95135}. 

We also achieve superior weighted average $R^2$ fitting accuracy across five additional well-known benchmarks, including Nguyen \cite{uy2011semantically}, Constant, Keijzer \cite{keijzer2003improving}, R rationals \cite{krawiec2013approximating}, and the AI-Feynman database \cite{udrescu2020ai}. In addition to the three transformer-based as outlined above, we compared with two additional online searching approaches, DSO \cite{petersen2019deep} and the standard GP-based symbolic regression \cite{koza1994genetic}. As detailed in the Appendix \ref{additional benchmarks}, our method outperforms  all compared methods with a weighted average $R^2$ of \textbf{0.95044} across these six benchmarks. Since on these benchmarks, the skeleton recovery rate has not been previously reported, our comparison is based solely on the weighted average $R^2$. 
All evaluations and baseline implementation were conducted following the setup of SymPointMLP \cite{li2022transformer}.

